<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/P 72 0 R
/T (Computational thinking is the ability to think and solve problems in a structured way, following a logic similar to that of a computer. It consists of breaking down complex problems into smaller parts, identifying patterns, forming general rules and designing step-by-step instructions to find effective solutions. This process involves analyzing the situation, generating possible strategies and evaluating their results, thus promoting logical and creative reasoning. The development of computational thinking from an early age promotes spatiotemporal orientation, abstract thinking and creative skills, stimulating imagination and problem solving.)
/E (Computational thinking is the ability to think and solve problems in a structured way, following a logic similar to that of a computer. It consists of breaking down complex problems into smaller parts, identifying patterns, forming general rules and designing step-by-step instructions to find effective solutions. This process involves analyzing the situation, generating possible strategies and evaluating their results, thus promoting logical and creative reasoning. The development of computational thinking from an early age promotes spatiotemporal orientation, abstract thinking and creative skills, stimulating imagination and problem solving.)
/Pg 9 0 R
/K [8 9]
>>
endobj
114 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/P 72 0 R
/T (This module introduces the concept of Computational Thinking and its main characteristics. Section 1: explores its educational potential and analyzes how to promote it in the classroom. Section 2: presents examples of disconnected and connected activities to work on it in a practical way. Finally, an overview of educational robots and the most suitable programming environments for each educational stage is offered.)
/E (This module introduces the concept of Computational Thinking and its main characteristics. Section 1: explores its educational potential and analyzes how to promote it in the classroom. Section 2: presents examples of disconnected and connected activities to work on it in a practical way. Finally, an overview of educational robots and the most suitable programming environments for each educational stage is offered.)
/Pg 9 0 R
/K [10 11]
>>
endobj
